  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days...\n\nOn the backside of the low that gave us scatted showers \nyesterday, some areas of fog will develop this morning. The main \narea for development wis SE Montana including Miles City and \nBaker. Some models are more aggressive with fog development than \nothers, however all show Baker being the main location for fog \nthis morning. Model soundings from nearly all short range models \ndepict a very good fog setup at KBHK with the surface temperature \ndecreasing well below the crossover temperature within the next \nfew hours. Additionally light winds and a very strong (10C) \ninversion will aid in fog development. As you progress father west\nand southwest the conditions gradually become less favorable. In \nshort, Baker could see dense fog this morning with Miles City \nseeing patchy fog, some of which may be dense in river valleys. \n\nToday, and through most of tomorrow, we will be under a brief \nperiod of ridging. This will allow for highs to remain above \naverage both days. Late Friday into early Saturday, our next\npotential weather maker moves into the region. The weak low that \nmoves up along the front range now looks like it will miss our CWA\njust to the east and therefore, not give us any precip. The good \nnews though is that this vort max will be close enough to help \nforce the shortwave moving in out of the west just north enough. \nThis should spare our CWA from a high wind event. Models now show \nthe 50kt 700mb jet passing through northern Montana with the \nstrongest modeled 700mb winds over south-centeral MT only \nreaching 30kts. These winds aloft, aided by a weak cold front, \nwill be enough to produce some breezy winds in the foothills of \nour western CWA. These winds are not expected to exceed 40mph, \neven in the most gap prone areas. \n\nThe next shortwave, progged to move into our area late Sunday /\nearly Monday, could be a different story. Models are depicting\nthis feature as a longwave trough just off of the Pacific Coast\nbefore splitting into two distinct areas of energy, one that\ncontinues to dig into the SW CONUS, and one that moves through \nour neck of the woods. This is why I refer to this feature as a \nshortwave, but it really is a disjointed longwave trough. Anyways,\nthe impacts to the Northern Rockies region will come primarily in\nthe form of precipitation. This system will take advantage of \nboth the surface moisture advecting out of the plains, and the \nmoisture aloft it drags in from the Pacific. This could bring \nanother round of decent precip amounts, although not as impressive\nas the storm several days ago. There are also hints that \naccumulating snow over 9kft could come with this event.\n\nAs we look into late next week, there is no good agreement \namongst models at all. What they do all show though is that we \nwill start to see deeper troughs that move through western North \nAmerica. These could be aided by remnants of several hurricanes \ncurrently in the East Pacific and those that will likely develop\nsoon. Simply, it is too early to say with any confidence what \nwill happen. It does appear though that the Northern Hemisphere \npolar jet is starting to take on more \"fall- like\"\ncharacteristics. WMR",
